WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.050
Why does automation need a boundary?

2
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.713
Because it cuts both ways.

3
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:08.679
On the speed side: Microsoft Learn recommends
the full remediation level for device

4
00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:13.433
groups, so threats on workstations are
remediated automatically, and Learn says

5
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:17.974
every automatic attack disruption action
can be undone by the security team.

6
00:00:17.974 --> 00:00:22.050
On the outage side: a contained
server is an outage, and a contained

7
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.275
emergency access account is a lockout,
in the middle of the incident.

8
00:00:26.275 --> 00:00:28.350
The guide names both as dangers.

9
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.940
And Learn itself cautions that
exclusions reduce protection,

10
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.150
so you can't exclude your way out either.

11
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.748
The Security Operations
and Telemetry book's

12
00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:39.825
response ladder decides what
may act without a person.

13
00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:44.731
This module sets Defender's own automation
to match it, with two settings: each

14
00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:46.877
device group's remediation level,

15
00:00:46.877 --> 00:00:50.300
and the exclusions for
automatic attack disruption.
